# Java JDK and runtime patch version policy

## Requirement

The JDK used to compile, test and run a Java product MUST be compatible with the approved Java runtime/toolchain baseline, allowing only authorized maintenance updates within that baseline's release line.

## Rules

- Record the approved JDK feature/release line and its exact baseline, including vendor when relevant.
- Permit only maintenance/security releases within that approved line, following the JDK vendor's version scheme; do not assume that JDK releases follow ordinary SemVer `major.minor.patch` semantics.
- Upgrading to another Java feature release requires an explicit baseline change.
- Verify the actual `java`, `javac`, test runtime and build toolchain versions used, rather than relying on a project declaration alone.
- Validate runtime/compiler compatibility, including bytecode target where relevant.
- Preserve verification evidence outside the release deliverable.

## Example

If Java feature release `25` is approved, a compatible maintenance release within the Java 25 line may be accepted; Java 26 is not an automatic replacement.

## Open decision

JDK vendor policy, exact approved JDK baseline and vendor-specific patch comparison must be defined in the relevant technology baseline.

